WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.540
Chicago officials hope to win a bid for
Amazon's second headquarters which might

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.190
launch them into the top tier of tech-friendly cities. So given that, why on

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.990
earth would Chicago's mayor propose a
tax on startups like Lyft and Uber? It

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:23.400
seems sort of anti-tech. I mean, I get the
basic reason: It's a way of raising

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.720
revenue for the city's public transit
system. It's becoming a vicious cycle:

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.949
public transit problems boost Uber and
Lyft, to the detriment of public transit.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:37.559
But taxing successful startups to prop
up failing transit systems isn't what's

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:41.489
best for tech or what's best for
consumers. Indeed, some other cities are

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:45.360
already finding value in cutting public
transit and instead subsidizing

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.440
ride-sharing. A better approach would be
to embrace new innovations at all

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.430
transit sectors. As Uber responded after
the tax was proposed,

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:57.030
"When safe and affordable rides are
available across every neighborhood –

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:02.609
whether by bus train or ride share –
Chicagoans can get to their jobs or

00:01:02.609 --> 00:01:05.320
family obligations without having to own
a car."

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.060
Do you think government regulations are too tough on tech? Let us know in your comments. Also, let us know

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.270
what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:20.840
and be sure to like and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

